    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d633">

                <pb facs="0830" n="742" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv02/pg742"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">600.119/1723a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Laughlin</hi>)<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d633-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-11-25T17:00:00-05:00"
                            >November 25, 1918—5 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3037. For Sheldon [from War Trade Board]: No. 1700. We should like to put into
                    effect a blanket license for the export of all commodities except certain
                    foodstuffs and possibly a few other articles for shipments to Great Britain,
                    France, Italy and their colonies. We are advising embassies here. Please discuss
                    with proper authorities and cable us their comments. We are putting into effect
                    blanket license for the export of cotton to Great Britain, France, Italy, and
                    Japan effective December 2nd.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d633-f1" n="17" type="source" xml:id="d633-f1-content">
                <p>The same, Nov. 23, 1918, to the Ambassador in Italy as No. 1859 (for Stewart, No.
                    45) (600.119/1776e). A similar telegram was sent on Nov. 23, 1918, to the
                    Ambassador in France as No. 6400 (for McFadden, No. 463) (611.5131/421).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
